<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Small, Brown Bag.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify key words and phrases in texts. Reading: Follow written instructions with visual aids. Phonemic Awareness: Identify a spoken word's initial and final phoneme if these words have been previously encountered and are attached to a picture.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify keywords and phrases in simple texts related to classroom objects (e.g., recognizing "desk," "book," and "pen" in the sentence, "The pen is on the desk next to the book."). Can follow written instructions to identify classroom objects (e.g., "Circle the picture of the pencil."). Can identify the initial and final phonemes of common classroom objects and make the correspondence between letters and their sounds at the beginning and end of words with visual support (e.g., identifying the initial letter and sound /p/ and the final letter and sound /l/ in "pencil" when shown a picture of a pencil).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activates prior knowledge and introduces the lesson's focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, class! How are you today?" (Sociolinguistic: greetings). The teacher shows real classroom objects (e.g., a big blue book, a small brown bag, a red pencil, a yellow ruler). The teacher holds up each object and asks, "What's this?" and "What color is it?" The students respond chorally, practicing vocabulary from Lesson 1 (e.g., "It's a book!", "It's blue!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a small brown bag and says, "This is a small, brown bag." (Grammar: adjectives, Present simple; Pragmatic: describing objects). Then holds up a big blue book and says, "That's a big, blue book." The teacher exaggerates the initial sounds /b/ in "book" and /s/ in "small" while pointing to the words on flashcards (Pronunciation: /b/, /s/). The teacher writes "small" and "big" on the board with pictures and points to the words as students repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher holds up a small bag and asks, "Is this bag big or small?" (Expected answer: "Small!"). The teacher holds up a red crayon and asks, "Is this crayon blue or red?" (Expected answer: "Red!").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario. Focuses on presenting a reading text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher presents a simple illustrated story or a set of descriptive sentences about classroom objects. The text is short and uses previously introduced vocabulary and grammar structures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated text on a large chart, projector, or individual worksheets. The text describes various classroom objects using adjectives (colors, sizes) and prepositions (on, in, under, next to, by). Example text: "This is a desk. It is big. A small, blue book is on the desk. Next to the book is a red pencil. That's a brown bag under the chair." (Grammar: Adjectives, Present Simple; Vocabulary: desk, book, pencil, bag, chair, big, small, blue, red, brown, on, next to, under).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Identify keywords and phrases in simple texts related to classroom objects. Pragmatic: Describing objects with detail: "This is a small bag." "That's a big, blue book." Responding to questions: "What color is the book?" "It’s blue."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the text aloud once, pointing to the words and corresponding pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are we reading about?" (Expected: "Classroom things!"). "Are there many or few things?" (Expected: "Many things!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students are given cut-out pictures of the objects mentioned in the text (e.g., small blue book, red pencil, brown bag). The teacher reads a sentence, "A small, blue book is on the desk," and students hold up the picture of the small blue book.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Keyword identification:</b> Students individually circle the words "book" and "bag" in their printed text/worksheet.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Focuses on accuracy in reading, practicing specific linguistic elements.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Identifying keywords and phrases, following written instructions. Grammar: Adjectives, Present simple ("That's a book."), prepositions (on, in, under, next to, by). Vocabulary: book, bag, pencil, desk, chair, eraser, ruler, board, marker, crayon, paper, shelf, folder, backpack, notebook, window, door, chalk, scissors, poster, table, bin, brown, red, blue, yellow, green, black, white, big, small. Phonemic awareness: Initial and final phonemes of target words (e.g., /p/ in pencil, /l/ in pencil).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Read and Circle.</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with simple sentences and two visual options. Students read the sentence and circle the picture that matches. For example:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This is a small, brown bag." (Picture of a small brown bag vs. a big blue bag)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The pencil is red." (Picture of a red pencil vs. a blue pencil)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher monitors and provides support for reading words and identifying initial/final sounds (e.g., pointing to 'p' in 'pencil' and asking "What sound is this?").</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Sentence Completion with Pictures.</b> Students receive a worksheet with a picture and an incomplete sentence. They read the sentence and complete it using a word from a given word bank (with visual cues if needed). For example:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of a blue book. Sentence: "That's a _____ book." (Word bank: red, blue, green)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of a pencil. Sentence: "This is a _____. What is the first sound? /_____/." (Word bank: crayon, ruler, pencil, p)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher explicitly guides students to identify the initial phoneme for a word like "pencil" (/p/) and "book" (/b/) after reading the word, showing the letter. </p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Read and Draw.</b> Students work individually. The teacher provides short, simple written instructions. Students read each instruction and draw the specified object in a designated space on their worksheet. For example:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a big, red apple."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a small, yellow ruler."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a brown bag."</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This activity focuses on following written instructions with visual output. The teacher circulates, observing reading and drawing, providing assistance with vocabulary or reading difficult words.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a realistic communicative task, focusing on fluency and task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "Classroom Object Scavenger Hunt." Students read a series of written clues to locate and identify classroom objects. The focus is on their ability to read and understand written instructions containing target vocabulary, adjectives, and prepositions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual or small group (2-3 students) activity using a worksheet with written instructions and visual aids. Students will complete the task at their desks or by briefly moving around the classroom if feasible.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains the "Classroom Object Scavenger Hunt." "Today, you will be detectives! You need to read the clues and find the objects in the picture (or classroom)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a worksheet with a drawing of a classroom scene or a picture with multiple classroom objects. Each instruction is numbered and includes a visual icon to aid understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example instructions on the worksheet:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Circle the small, brown bag.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Put an 'X' on the big, blue book on the desk.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Draw a red crayon next to the pencil.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Find the green eraser under the chair. (Students point to it or circle it).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Color the board black.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models the first instruction: "Look at number 1. 'Circle the small, brown bag.' I will circle the small, brown bag here." (Teacher circles it on a projected example or large poster).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work independently or in pairs to read the instructions and perform the actions on their worksheet. The teacher circulates, offering help with reading unfamiliar words and observing how students follow the instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage polite expressions if students are working in pairs: "Can you please show me the blue book?" or "Thank you for finding the red crayon." (Sociolinguistic: polite expressions).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Classroom Object Scavenger Hunt." The teacher will note students' ability to read keywords, understand phrases, and correctly follow instructions. The completed worksheets will serve as a product for evaluation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' completed "Classroom Object Scavenger Hunt" worksheet from Stage 4. They will have demonstrated their ability to read instructions and identify/manipulate objects based on the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify keywords (e.g., "bag," "book," "pencil," colors, sizes) in written instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow simple written instructions (e.g., "Circle," "Draw," "Put an X").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows understanding of initial/final phonemes (e.g., identifying /p/ and /l/ in 'pencil' when prompted by the teacher or an instruction like "Write the first sound of 'pencil'").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper slip with two simple statements or emojis to circle.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can read about colors and sizes of classroom objects. (😊 / 😐 / 😞)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can follow written instructions to find things. (😊 / 😐 / 😞)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Learner and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> The teacher asks students to think about their learning.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words did you read today?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult when reading the instructions?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., visual aids, TPR for instructions, repetition of keywords, phonemic awareness practice).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction)? (e.g., more practice with prepositions, clearer instructions for group work, additional phonemic awareness activities if needed).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did all students meet the learning outcome for reading keywords and following instructions? How can I support those who struggled?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and identifying classroom objects. In Lesson 3, students will use this vocabulary and grammar to speak and interact, asking and answering questions about classroom objects' location, color, and size (e.g., "Where is the blue book?" "It's on the desk.").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>